  "slug": "interaction-gene-9a09",
  "summary": "Speed and memory limits in sequencing workflows are being addressed across read handling, nanopore classification, variant calling, and polishing.",
  "synthesis": "The four items share a focus on throughput and resource efficiency in sequencing data processing rather than a single method class. FastDedup, a Rust-based FASTX deduplicator using xxh3 hash fingerprints, targets PCR duplicate removal with low memory use and is reported to outperform six tools on synthetic data. NanoLabel is a real-time nanopore signal classifier for adaptive sampling, built on RawHash2 with target-region mapping and a lightweight feature-based component. The Clair3 report describes accelerated long-read variant calling with parallel feature generation, in-memory haplotagging and GPU inference, reaching 30x WGS in 12-20 minutes. AIEdit is an alignment-free, neural-network-based assembly polisher trained on spaced seed match patterns that reduces error rates faster than alignment-based tools. The novelty lies mainly in replacing alignment or heavyweight steps with hashing, lightweight features, parallelization or alignment-free learning.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-05-09"
}
